\f19 , you already must have realized that Latin resembled in no way the series of monotonous sounds with the virtues of a sleeping pill that you had probably experienced before.\par \par But if you want to look at things more closely, you will notice not only that a correct pronunciation and accentuation give the language its }{\i\f21 life}{\f19  and its }{\i\f21 sense}{\f19 , but also that the pronunciation called }{\i\f21 restored}{\f19 , which we have adopted, is the }{\i\f21 simplest of European pronunciations}{\f19 . In effect, disregarding minimal exceptions:\par \par }{\b\f20 All letters are pronounced.\par For every letter there corresponds only a single sound.\par For every sound there corresponds only a single letter.\par }{\f19 \par What more can one ask for?\par \par }{\i\f21 Note.}{\f19 \emdash  If you are still not sure of your pronunciation, refer one more time to Avitus's files.\par \par }{\b\f20 3.}{\f19  }{\b\f20 The conjugations.}{\f19 \emdash  We have already seen in Lesson 4, the complete conjugation of the present indicative of the verb e}{\b\f20 sse}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 to be}{\f19  (phrase 10), as well as that of its compounds, }{\b\f20 ad}{\f19 e}{\b\f20 sse}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 to be present}{\f19 , and }{\b\f20 ab}{\f19 e}{\b\f20 sse}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 to be absent}{\f19 . If you know French already, or any other Romance language, you will have no difficulty in recalling this, because they resemble the conjugation of the corresponding verbs to be (French: \u234\'90tre). Let us review it once again:\par \par (}{\b\f20 e}{\f19 go) }{\b\f20 sum }{\f19 [}{\i\f21 French: je}{\b\i\f22  suis}{\f19 ]: }{\i\f21 I }{\b\i\f22 am\par }{\f19 (tu) }{\b\f20 es }{\f19 [}{\i\f21 French: tu}{\b\i\f22  es}{\f19 ]: }{\i\f21 you }{\b\i\f22 are\par }{\f19 (}{\b\f20 i}{\f19 lle) }{\b\f20 est }{\f19 [}{\i\f21 French: il}{\b\i\f22  est}{\f19 ]: }{\i\f21 he/she/it }{\b\i\f22 is\par }{\f19 (nos) }{\b\f20 s}{\f19 u}{\b\f20 mus}{\f19  [}{\i\f21 French: nous}{\b\i\f22  sommes}{\f19 ]: }{\i\f21 we }{\b\i\f22 are\par }{\f19 (vos) e}{\b\f20 stis}{\f19  [}{\i\f21 French: vous}{\b\i\f22  \u234\'90tes}{\i\f21 ;}{\b\i\f22  }{\i\f21 Old French }{\b\i\f22 estes}{\f19 ]: }{\i\f21 you[pl.] }{\b\i\f22 are\par }{\f19 (}{\b\f20 i}{\f19 lli) }{\b\f20 sunt}{\f19  [}{\i\f21 French: ils}{\b\i\f22  sont}{\f19 ]: }{\i\f21 they }{\b\i\f22 are\par }{\f19 \par N.B.\emdash  We put the Latin pronouns in brackets above, because they are only used when we want to stress the identity of the subject: }{\i\f21 *I* am}{\f19 , etc\u8230\'c9 (cf. L 2, N 1).\par \par From this verb, try to recall the endings:\par \par }{\b\f20 m }{\f19 (or}{\b\f20  o}{\f19 ) for the 1}{\f19\super st}{\f19  person singular\par }{\b\f20 s}{\f19  2}{\f19\super nd}{\f19 \par }{\b\f20 t}{\f19  3}{\f19\super rd}{\f19 \par }{\b\f20 mus }{\f19 1}{\f19\super st}{\f19  plural\par }{\b\f20 tis }{\f19 2}{\f19\super nd}{\f19 \par }{\b\f20 nt }{\f19 3}{\f19\super rd}{\f19 \par \par Now take the Latin verb }{\b\f20 ven}{\f19 i}{\b\f20 re}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 to come}{\f19 , which we saw in Lesson 2 under the forms }{\b\f20 v}{\f19 e}{\b\f20 nio}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 I come}{\f19 , and }{\b\f20 v}{\f19 e}{\b\f20 nit}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 he comes}{\f19 . You will obtain without difficulty its conjugation by using the preceding endings:\par \par }{\i\f21 Singular:}{\f19  }{\b\f20 v}{\f19 e}{\b\f20 niO}{\f19 , }{\b\f20 v}{\f19 e}{\b\f20 niS}{\f19 , }{\b\f20 v}{\f19 e}{\b\f20 niT}{\f19 : }{\i\f21 I come}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 you come}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 he/she/it comes}{\f19 .\par }{\i\f21 Plural:}{\f19  }{\b\f20 ven}{\f19 i}{\b\f20 MUS}{\f19 , }{\b\f20 ven}{\f19 i}{\b\f20 TIS}{\f19 , }{\b\f20 v}{\f19 e}{\b\f20 niUNT}{\f19 : }{\i\f21 we come}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 you[pl.] come}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 they come}{\f19 .\par \par In the same way for the verb }{\b\f20 d}{\f19 a}{\b\f20 re}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 to give}{\f19 , as we saw in the same lesson:\par \par }{\i\f21 Singular:}{\f19  }{\b\f20 dO}{\f19 , }{\b\f20 daS}{\f19 , }{\b\f20 daT}{\f19 : }{\i\f21 I give}{\f19 , }{\i\f21 you\u8230\'c9}{\f19\lang1036 , etc.\par }{\i\f21\lang1036 Plural:}{\f19\lang1036  }{\b\f20\lang1036 d}{\f19\lang1036 a}{\b\f20\lang1036 MUS}{\f19\lang1036 , }{\b\f20\lang1036 d}{\f19\lang1036 a}{\b\f20\lang1036 TIS}{\f19\lang1036 , }{\b\f20\lang1036 daNT}{\f19\lang1036 : }{\i\f21\lang1036 we give}{\f19\lang1036 , }{\i\f21\lang1036 you[pl.]\u8230\'c9}{\f19\lang1036 , etc.\par }{\f19 \par N.B.\emdash  We shall see the verbs in }{\b\f20 or}{\f19 , }{\b\f20 ris}{\f19 , etc., in the upcoming review lesson.\par \par }{\b\f20 4.}{\f19  To finish this revision, practice pronouncing correctly the captions imbedded in the illustrations for these first seven lessons. All of these captions are sentences taken from the preceding lessons and exercises. You will not have any difficulty in finding their meaning, if }{\f19 by chance you had forgotten it.}{\par }}